Classical Japanese Zen: reading Dōgen (@buddhist-dogen: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @buddhist-further-reading, #source @buddhist-source-dogen, #philosophy @buddhist-research-study, #buddhism @buddhist-research-study) { r_06M92K0Y27WRJY7K34A0YFTDJN
**Read:** Begin after an introduction to Zen, with the editor’s orientation and the fascicles Genjōkōan and Uji as a bounded first study; use the edition’s translated titles. Read complete fascicles, then extend to those the investigation needs. This selection is not the complete Shōbōgenzō.

**Investigate:** Track an image, an allusion, and an argument about practice or time. Compare translations when the interpretation turns on wording. Reconstruct how a difficult sentence works in the complete fascicle, and distinguish a suggestive paraphrase from a defended reading. Deepening can expand through the collection without inventing a claim to have read it all.

**Check:** identify the passages, edition, and interpretation another reader would need to examine your account. Let the comparison modify an earlier view when the evidence warrants it.

**Source:** [Treasury of the True Dharma Eye — Shōbōgenzō](../sources/Dogen.lingua#r_4DEXWD8M2MBXYZZP18XZHA7QM7).

